Here is what I found across the repository. This is a Spring PetClinic variant that has BOTH a Spring arm and an OfficeFloor (net.officefloor) REST arm. Note upfront: `customerCode` does not exist anywhere in the production code, DTOs, or DB schema — it appears ONLY in an acceptance test that expects it to be implemented.

## 1. Owner entity/model class

Path: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`

`@Entity @Table(name = "owners")`, extends `Person`. Fields: `address`, `city`, `telephone` (E.164 validated), `email`, `registrationDate` (LocalDate), `pets` (OneToMany). NO `customerCode` field. Key excerpt:

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "owners")
public class Owner extends Person {
    @Column(name = "address") @NotEmpty private String address;
    @Column(name = "city") @NotEmpty private String city;
    @Column(name = "telephone") @NotEmpty
    @Pattern(regexp = "^\\+[0-9]{8,15}$", message = "Phone number must be E.164, e.g. +61412345678")
    private String telephone;
    @Column(name = "email") private String email;
    @Column(name = "registration_date", columnDefinition = "DATE") private LocalDate registrationDate;
    @OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL, mappedBy = "owner", fetch = FetchType.EAGER) private Set<Pet> pets;
    ...
    public void setEmail(String email) { this.email = (email == null) ? null : email.toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT); }
}
```

Parent `Person` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Person.java`) is a `@MappedSuperclass` with `firstName` and `lastName`; it extends `BaseEntity` (which supplies `id`).

## 2. OfficeFloor REST YAML for POST owners

Path: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`

A 4-step function chain (build → ensureUniqueTelephone → save → respond), all under `transaction` governance, authorized by `hasRole('OWNER_ADMIN')`:

```yaml
composition:
  authorize: "hasRole('OWNER_ADMIN')"
build:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.BuildOwner
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: ensureUniqueTelephone
ensureUniqueTelephone:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.EnsureOwnerTelephoneUnique
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: save
save:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.SaveOwner
  govern: [ transaction ]
  next: respond
respond:
  class: org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.function.owner.RespondWithOwnerCreated
  govern: [ transaction ]
```

Referenced function classes (all under `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/`):

- `BuildOwner.java` — takes `@Valid @RequestBody OwnerFieldsDto`, normalizes telephone via `E164.normalize(...)`, maps to `Owner` via `OwnerMapper.toOwner(...)`, and defaults `registrationDate` to `LocalDate.now()` when null. Outputs `Out<Owner>`.
- `EnsureOwnerTelephoneUnique.java` — loads ALL owners via `ownerRepository.findAll().stream()` and throws `DuplicateTelephoneException` if the telephone already exists. (Relevant to counting/sequence logic — it iterates the full owner set.)
- `SaveOwner.java` — `ownerRepository.save(owner)`.
- `RespondWithOwnerCreated.java` — maps to `OwnerDto` via `ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner)` and returns `ResponseEntity.created(URI.create("/api/owners/" + owner.getId())).body(dto)`.

Other owner functions in that dir (context): `ListOwners`, `ListOwnersPage`, `LoadOwner`, `ApplyOwner`, `ValidateOwner`, `RespondWithOwner`, `RespondWithOwnerUpdated`, `DeleteOwner`, `BuildOwnersPet`, `LoadOwnersPet`, `E164`.

## 3. OwnerRepository interface

Path: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/OwnerRepository.java`

Methods: `findByLastName(String)`, `findByLastName(String, Pageable)`, `findById(int)`, `save(Owner)`, `findAll()`, `findAll(Pageable)`, `delete(Owner)`. There is NO `count()` method. Signatures:

```java
Collection<Owner> findByLastName(String lastName) throws DataAccessException;
Page<Owner> findByLastName(String lastName, Pageable pageable) throws DataAccessException;
Owner findById(int id) throws DataAccessException;
void save(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException;
Collection<Owner> findAll() throws DataAccessException;
Page<Owner> findAll(Pageable pageable) throws DataAccessException;
void delete(Owner owner) throws DataAccessException;
```

Implementations: `repository/jdbc/JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl.java`, `repository/jpa/JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl.java`, `repository/springdatajpa/SpringDataOwnerRepository.java`. The Spring Data one uses `COUNT(owner)` only inside paging `countQuery` values (no public `count()` on Owner). Note `JdbcPetRepositoryImpl` and `SpringDataPetRepository` do have `count(*)`/`count(pet)` for pets — but not for owners.

## 4. Tests related to creating owners (POST /owners)

Two files under `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/`:

`AcceptanceTest.java` — this is the test that references `customerCode` (which is NOT yet implemented). It expects a derived `customerCode` field of format `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` (upper-cased first 3 letters of last name, hyphen, a zero-padded global sequence number):

```java
/** customer-code: Assign 'customerCode' formatted '<LAST3>-<NNNN>' where LAST3 is the upper-cased first thre... */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {
    @Test
    void coreFormatAndGlobalSequence() throws Exception {
        ObjectNode a = ownerNode(); a.put("lastName", "smithers");
        int ida = createOwnerOk(a);
        ObjectNode b = ownerNode(); b.put("lastName", "jones");
        int idb = createOwnerOk(b);
        String ca = fetchOwner(ida).get("customerCode").asText();
        String cb = fetchOwner(idb).get("customerCode").asText();
        assertTrue(ca.startsWith("SMI-"), ca);
        assertTrue(cb.startsWith("JON-"), cb);
        assertEquals(Integer.parseInt(ca.substring(4)) + 1, Integer.parseInt(cb.substring(4)));
    }
}
```

So the expected `customerCode` = first 3 letters of lastName upper-cased + `-` + a global incrementing 4-digit sequence (the two created owners get consecutive numbers). Note `substring(4)` implies exactly 3 letters + hyphen before the number.

`AcceptanceBase.java` — black-box base class. Runs `@SpringBootTest @AutoConfigureMockMvc @Transactional @WithMockUser(roles={"OWNER_ADMIN","VET_ADMIN","ADMIN"})`. Every test hits `POST /api/owners` and reads back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`. Key helpers:

- `ownerNode()` builds a fully-valid unique payload with `firstName, lastName, address, city, telephone` (letters-only names, telephone `04%08d`).
- `createOwner(body)` → `POST /api/owners`; `createOwnerOk(body)` asserts 2xx and returns the id (via response body `id` or `Location` header).
- `fetchOwner(id)` → `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns parsed JSON.
- `extractId(...)` parses `id` from body or trailing segment of `Location`.
- Also helpers hinting at further planned rules: `createOwnerWithKey` (Idempotency-Key header), `fillCity`, `createMany`, `deleteOwner` (soft-delete), plus pinned region/postcode/timezone/holiday reference data and `sha256hex`/`shaHex`/`luhn` utilities.

## 5. Request/response DTO classes for owners

The DTOs are NOT hand-written source files — they are generated from OpenAPI. Referenced types: `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto.OwnerFieldsDto` (request body), `org.springframework.samples.petclinic.rest.dto.OwnerDto` (response), and `OwnerPageDto`. They are generated at build time from:

Path: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml`

- `OwnerFields` (lines ~1991-2052) — properties `firstName, lastName, address, city, telephone, email, registrationDate`; required = firstName, lastName, address, city, telephone. NO `customerCode`.
- `Owner` (lines ~2053-2088) — `allOf` OwnerFields plus read-only `id`, `displayName`, `initials`, and `pets` (required). NO `customerCode`.
- `OwnerPage` (~2089+) — content/page/size/totalElements/totalPages.

Mapping is done by MapStruct at `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`, which adds computed `displayName` and `initials` via expressions, `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` (ignores id and pets), and `toOwnerPageDto`.

To add `customerCode` you would add it to the `Owner` (and/or `OwnerFields`) schema in openapi.yml so the generated DTO carries it, plus map it.

## 6. Database schema — no customerCode column

Schema files exist per-DB under `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/db/{h2,hsqldb,postgres,mysql}/schema.sql` (+ `data.sql`), selected by `spring.sql.init.platform` in `application-{h2,hsqldb,postgres,mysql}.properties`.

- `db/hsqldb/schema.sql` owners table: `id, first_name, last_name, address, city, telephone, email, registration_date` — no customer_code.
- `db/h2/schema.sql` owners table is even more minimal: `id, first_name, last_name, address, city, telephone` (NOT NULL) — no email/registration_date, no customer_code.

The Owner entity uses JPA `@Column` annotations (see item 1). There is NO `customerCode`/`customer_code` column anywhere in any schema.sql, nor a column annotation on the entity.

## Summary of the gap
`customerCode` is required only by `AcceptanceTest.coreFormatAndGlobalSequence`. To satisfy it you would need to: add the field to the `Owner` entity + DB schema(s), add it to the `Owner` OpenAPI schema (so `OwnerDto` exposes it), and compute the `<LAST3>-<NNNN>` value with a global sequence during owner creation. The OfficeFloor create path is the 4 function classes in `.../rest/function/owner/` driven by `owners.POST.yml`; the Spring arm's equivalent would live in the same repository/mapper/DTO layer. There is currently no `count()` on `OwnerRepository`, so the "global sequence" would need a new count/sequence source (existing code approximates full-set access only via `findAll()`, as seen in `EnsureOwnerTelephoneUnique`).

